         {
           "source": "def-explicit-runge-kutta-tableau-and-stage-map",
           "source_section": "Definition",
-          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$ be an open time-state domain, and let $f:D\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be the right-hand side of a first-order initial-value problem in the sense of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]. An explicit $s$-stage Runge–Kutta tableau consists of a positive integer $s$, real coefficients $a_{ij}$, $b_i$, and $c_i$ with $a_{ij}=0$ whenever $j\\ge i$, and\n$$c_i=\\sum_{j<i}a_{ij}\\qquad(1\\le i\\le s).$$\nThe finite row sum is empty, hence $c_1=0$.",
+          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$ be an open time-state domain, and let $f:D\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be the right-hand side of a first-order initial-value problem in the sense of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]. An explicit $s$-stage Runge–Kutta tableau consists of a positive integer $s$, real coefficients $a_{ij}$, $b_i$, and $c_i$ with $a_{ij}=0$ whenever $j\\ge i$, and\n$$c_i=\\sum_{j<i}a_{ij}\\qquad(1\\le i\\le s).$$\nThe finite row sum is empty, hence $c_1=0$.\n\nFor a step size $h>0$ from an admitted base pair $(t,y)\\in D$, compute in order\n$$Y_i=y+h\\sum_{j<i}a_{ij}K_j,\\qquad t_i=t+c_i h,\\qquad K_i=f(t_i,Y_i)\\quad(i=1,\\ldots,s).$$\nThe strict lower-triangular condition makes each stage depend only on already computed slopes; no implicit stage equation is solved. If every stage is defined, the candidate output is\n$$y^+=y+h\\sum_{i=1}^s b_iK_i.$$\n\nThe procedure accepts an exact right-hand-side evaluation oracle that returns either the value $f(t_i,Y_i)$ or an evaluation-failure status. Before each query it checks $(t_i,Y_i)\\in D$. It returns domain-failure with the first failing stage if that check fails, evaluation-failure with the stage index if the oracle fails, and domain-failure at output if $(t+h,y^+)\\notin D$. Otherwise it returns success with $(t+h,y^+)$. In the exact mathematical model the oracle returns $f$ at every point of $D$; the failure branch records only an unavailable numerical evaluation. On success this is a one-step map in the sense of [[def-one-step-ode-method-local-defect-order-and-stability]].",
           "role": "Exact supplier excerpt linked outside the Facts block; contextual or definitional use only."
         },
         {
           "source": "def-one-step-ode-method-local-defect-order-and-stability",
           "source_section": "Definition",
-          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$ be open, and consider a first-order IVP and solution in the sense of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]. A **one-step method** is a family of maps $\\Psi_h(t,v)$, defined for admitted times $t$, positive step sizes $h$, and states $v$, whose value is the method's approximation at time $t+h$ from the single input state $v$ at time $t$. It does not use earlier numerical states.",
+          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $D\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$ be open, and consider a first-order IVP and solution in the sense of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]. A **one-step method** is a family of maps $\\Psi_h(t,v)$, defined for admitted times $t$, positive step sizes $h$, and states $v$, whose value is the method's approximation at time $t+h$ from the single input state $v$ at time $t$. It does not use earlier numerical states.\n\nIf $y$ is an exact solution on an interval containing $[t,t+h]$, the **local defect** is\n\n$$\\delta_h(t;y):=\\Psi_h(t,y(t))-y(t+h).$$\n\nLet $K\\subseteq D$ be a fixed compact time-state set. The method has **uniform local order $p$ on $K$ along $y$** if there are constants $C\\ge0$ and $h_0>0$, independent of the admitted starting time $t$ and step $0<h\\le h_0$, such that\n\n$$\\|\\delta_h(t;y)\\|_2\\le C h^{p+1}$$\n\nwhenever the exact solution graph on $[t,t+h]$ and the associated method time-state inputs, stages, and outputs under consideration lie in $K$. Here $p$ is a positive integer and $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$ is the Euclidean norm of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]].\n\nThe method has **one-step Lipschitz stability on $K$** with constant $L\\ge0$ if\n\n$$\\|\\Psi_h(t,u)-\\Psi_h(t,v)\\|_2\\le(1+Lh)\\|u-v\\|_2$$\n\nfor every admitted step size $h$, admitted time $t$, and input states $u,v$ with $(t,u),(t,v)\\in K$. This is a bound on the change in one step; it is separate from absolute or linear stability regions and does not itself assert a global error estimate.",
